2025-06-12 10:48:54,644 - INFO - logger_config.logger_config - Agent responded with message: content='Here is the recipe for Paneer Butter Masala:\n\n## Paneer Butter Masala (Restaurant Style)\n\nThis recipe aims to recreate the rich and creamy taste of Paneer Butter Masala you find in restaurants.\n\n**Yields:** 4 servings\n**Prep time:** 20 minutes\n**Cook time:** 40 minutes\n\n**Ingredients:**\n\n**For the Paneer:**\n\n*   250g Paneer (Indian Cheese), cut into 1-inch cubes\n*   1 tbsp Oil for shallow frying (optional, but recommended for better texture)\n\n**For the Tomato-Cashew Gravy:**\n\n*   2 tbsp Butter\n*   1 medium Onion, finely chopped\n*   1 tbsp Ginger-Garlic Paste\n*   2 medium Tomatoes, roughly chopped\n*   1/2 cup Cashews\n*   1-2 Green Chilies, slit (adjust to taste)\n*   1/2 tsp Turmeric Powder\n*   1 tsp Red Chili Powder (adjust to taste)\n*   1 tsp Coriander Powder\n*   1/2 tsp Garam Masala\n*   1/2 tsp Dried Fenugreek Leaves (Kasuri Methi), crushed\n*   1/2 cup Water (or as needed to blend)\n*   1/2 cup Heavy Cream or Fresh Cream\n*   Salt to taste\n*   Sugar (a pinch, optional to balance acidity)\n\n**For the Butter Masala Sauce:**\n\n*   2 tbsp Butter\n*   1/2 tsp Red Chili Powder (for color and flavor)\n*   1/4 tsp Garam Masala\n*   1 tbsp Fresh Cream\n*   Fresh Coriander Leaves, chopped for garnish\n\n**Instructions:**\n\n**1. Prepare the Paneer (Optional):**\n\n*   Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Gently shallow fry the paneer cubes until lightly golden brown on all sides. This helps to prevent them from crumbling in the gravy.\n*   Remove the paneer and set aside. You can also skip this step and add the paneer directly to the gravy, but frying improves texture.\n\n**2. Make the Tomato-Cashew Gravy:**\n\n*   Heat 2 tbsp butter in a pan or pot over medium heat.\n*   Add the chopped onion and sauté until golden brown, about 5-7 minutes.\n*   Add the ginger-garlic paste and green chilies and sauté for another minute until fragrant.\n*   Add the chopped tomatoes, cashews, turmeric powder, red chili powder, coriander powder, and salt. Mix well.\n*   Add 1/2 cup water and bring to a simmer. Cover and cook for 10-15 minutes, or until the tomatoes are soft and mushy.\n*   Let the mixture cool slightly.\n*   Transfer the mixture to a blender or food processor and blend until smooth. If needed, add a little more water to achieve a smooth consistency.\n*   Strain the blended gravy through a fine-mesh sieve back into the pan. This step ensures a silky smooth gravy.\n\n**3. Make the Butter Masala Sauce:**\n\n*   Return the strained gravy to the pan. Heat over low heat.\n*   Add 2 tbsp butter, red chili powder (for color), garam masala, and crushed kasuri methi. Mix well and simmer for 2-3 minutes.\n*   Add the shallow fried (or unfried) paneer cubes to the gravy. Gently stir to coat the paneer with the sauce.\n*   Add the heavy cream (or fresh cream) and a pinch of sugar (if using). Mix gently and simmer for another 2-3 minutes, allowing the paneer to absorb the flavors.\n*   Adjust salt to taste.\n\n**4. Serve:**\n\n*   Garnish with fresh coriander leaves and a swirl of fresh cream.\n*   Serve hot with naan, roti, rice, or paratha.\n\n**Tips and Variations:**\n\n*   **Paneer Quality:** Use fresh, good-quality paneer for the best results. If using store-bought paneer, soak it in warm water for 15-20 minutes to soften it before using.\n*   **Cashews:** Soaking the cashews in warm water for 30 minutes before blending will help them blend more smoothly.\n*   **Cream:** Heavy cream will give a richer flavor, but fresh cream works well too. You can also use half-and-half for a lighter option.\n*   **Spice Level:** Adjust the amount of red chili powder and green chilies to your preferred spice level.\n*   **Smoked Flavor:** For a smoky flavor, you can give the gravy a "dhungar" treatment. Place a small steel bowl in the center of the pan of gravy. Heat a piece of charcoal on a direct flame until it\'s red hot. Place the hot charcoal in the bowl, add a few drops of oil or ghee to the charcoal, and immediately cover the pan with a lid. Let it smoke for 2-3 minutes. Remove the bowl and discard the charcoal.\n*   **Vegan Option:** Substitute the paneer with tofu (press the tofu to remove excess water before using). Use vegan butter and cashew cream instead of dairy cream.\n*   **Ginger-Garlic Paste:** You can use store-bought ginger-garlic paste, but freshly made paste will have a better flavor.\n\nEnjoy your delicious homemade Paneer Butter Masala!' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'prompt_feedback': {'block_reason': 0, 'safety_ratings': []}, 'finish_reason': 'STOP', 'model_name': 'gemini-2.0-flash', 'safety_ratings': []} name='You are llm expert' id='run-832c6624-8349-4eda-940d-e538849a0015-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1789, 'output_tokens': 1139, 'total_tokens': 2928, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 0}}
2025-06-12 10:48:54,646 - INFO - logger_config.logger_config - Recipe agent result: content='Here is the recipe for Paneer Butter Masala:\n\n## Paneer Butter Masala (Restaurant Style)\n\nThis recipe aims to recreate the rich and creamy taste of Paneer Butter Masala you find in restaurants.\n\n**Yields:** 4 servings\n**Prep time:** 20 minutes\n**Cook time:** 40 minutes\n\n**Ingredients:**\n\n**For the Paneer:**\n\n*   250g Paneer (Indian Cheese), cut into 1-inch cubes\n*   1 tbsp Oil for shallow frying (optional, but recommended for better texture)\n\n**For the Tomato-Cashew Gravy:**\n\n*   2 tbsp Butter\n*   1 medium Onion, finely chopped\n*   1 tbsp Ginger-Garlic Paste\n*   2 medium Tomatoes, roughly chopped\n*   1/2 cup Cashews\n*   1-2 Green Chilies, slit (adjust to taste)\n*   1/2 tsp Turmeric Powder\n*   1 tsp Red Chili Powder (adjust to taste)\n*   1 tsp Coriander Powder\n*   1/2 tsp Garam Masala\n*   1/2 tsp Dried Fenugreek Leaves (Kasuri Methi), crushed\n*   1/2 cup Water (or as needed to blend)\n*   1/2 cup Heavy Cream or Fresh Cream\n*   Salt to taste\n*   Sugar (a pinch, optional to balance acidity)\n\n**For the Butter Masala Sauce:**\n\n*   2 tbsp Butter\n*   1/2 tsp Red Chili Powder (for color and flavor)\n*   1/4 tsp Garam Masala\n*   1 tbsp Fresh Cream\n*   Fresh Coriander Leaves, chopped for garnish\n\n**Instructions:**\n\n**1. Prepare the Paneer (Optional):**\n\n*   Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Gently shallow fry the paneer cubes until lightly golden brown on all sides. This helps to prevent them from crumbling in the gravy.\n*   Remove the paneer and set aside. You can also skip this step and add the paneer directly to the gravy, but frying improves texture.\n\n**2. Make the Tomato-Cashew Gravy:**\n\n*   Heat 2 tbsp butter in a pan or pot over medium heat.\n*   Add the chopped onion and sauté until golden brown, about 5-7 minutes.\n*   Add the ginger-garlic paste and green chilies and sauté for another minute until fragrant.\n*   Add the chopped tomatoes, cashews, turmeric powder, red chili powder, coriander powder, and salt. Mix well.\n*   Add 1/2 cup water and bring to a simmer. Cover and cook for 10-15 minutes, or until the tomatoes are soft and mushy.\n*   Let the mixture cool slightly.\n*   Transfer the mixture to a blender or food processor and blend until smooth. If needed, add a little more water to achieve a smooth consistency.\n*   Strain the blended gravy through a fine-mesh sieve back into the pan. This step ensures a silky smooth gravy.\n\n**3. Make the Butter Masala Sauce:**\n\n*   Return the strained gravy to the pan. Heat over low heat.\n*   Add 2 tbsp butter, red chili powder (for color), garam masala, and crushed kasuri methi. Mix well and simmer for 2-3 minutes.\n*   Add the shallow fried (or unfried) paneer cubes to the gravy. Gently stir to coat the paneer with the sauce.\n*   Add the heavy cream (or fresh cream) and a pinch of sugar (if using). Mix gently and simmer for another 2-3 minutes, allowing the paneer to absorb the flavors.\n*   Adjust salt to taste.\n\n**4. Serve:**\n\n*   Garnish with fresh coriander leaves and a swirl of fresh cream.\n*   Serve hot with naan, roti, rice, or paratha.\n\n**Tips and Variations:**\n\n*   **Paneer Quality:** Use fresh, good-quality paneer for the best results. If using store-bought paneer, soak it in warm water for 15-20 minutes to soften it before using.\n*   **Cashews:** Soaking the cashews in warm water for 30 minutes before blending will help them blend more smoothly.\n*   **Cream:** Heavy cream will give a richer flavor, but fresh cream works well too. You can also use half-and-half for a lighter option.\n*   **Spice Level:** Adjust the amount of red chili powder and green chilies to your preferred spice level.\n*   **Smoked Flavor:** For a smoky flavor, you can give the gravy a "dhungar" treatment. Place a small steel bowl in the center of the pan of gravy. Heat a piece of charcoal on a direct flame until it\'s red hot. Place the hot charcoal in the bowl, add a few drops of oil or ghee to the charcoal, and immediately cover the pan with a lid. Let it smoke for 2-3 minutes. Remove the bowl and discard the charcoal.\n*   **Vegan Option:** Substitute the paneer with tofu (press the tofu to remove excess water before using). Use vegan butter and cashew cream instead of dairy cream.\n*   **Ginger-Garlic Paste:** You can use store-bought ginger-garlic paste, but freshly made paste will have a better flavor.\n\nEnjoy your delicious homemade Paneer Butter Masala!' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'prompt_feedback': {'block_reason': 0, 'safety_ratings': []}, 'finish_reason': 'STOP', 'model_name': 'gemini-2.0-flash', 'safety_ratings': []} name='You are llm expert' id='run-832c6624-8349-4eda-940d-e538849a0015-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1789, 'output_tokens': 1139, 'total_tokens': 2928, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 0}}
