2025-06-12 10:49:17,512 - INFO - logger_config.logger_config - Agent invocation complete. Result: {'messages': [HumanMessage(content='You are provided from complete message history use it for user response\n        Human Feedback: content=\'Machine learning \' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={} id=\'79b21268-b74c-498c-beda-76e3318132a1\'\n        user query: content=\'what is \' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={} id=\'dcbd3b29-7783-4bfe-9131-00516d675c38\'\n        history : [HumanMessage(content=\'recommend spicy dishes \', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id=\'74859689-4b8a-48cc-8e8d-5c58fcdbb9a2\'), AIMessage(content=\'Okay, I can recommend some spicy dishes for you: Chicken Vindaloo, Spicy Ramen, Phaal Curry, Sichuan Hot Pot, Mexican Tacos with Salsa, and Buffalo Wings.\', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={\'prompt_feedback\': {\'block_reason\': 0, \'safety_ratings\': []}, \'finish_reason\': \'STOP\', \'model_name\': \'gemini-2.0-flash\', \'safety_ratings\': []}, name=\'You are llm expert\', id=\'run-993250b3-5b0e-4528-98bb-9686695f67ae-0\', usage_metadata={\'input_tokens\': 933, \'output_tokens\': 36, \'total_tokens\': 969, \'input_token_details\': {\'cache_read\': 0}}), HumanMessage(content=\'give me an recipe of paneer butter masala\', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id=\'948a3b4a-11f0-4cf1-b743-8448b50821a6\'), AIMessage(content=\'Here is the recipe for Paneer Butter Masala:\\n\\n## Paneer Butter Masala (Restaurant Style)\\n\\nThis recipe aims to recreate the rich and creamy taste of Paneer Butter Masala you find in restaurants.\\n\\n**Yields:** 4 servings\\n**Prep time:** 20 minutes\\n**Cook time:** 40 minutes\\n\\n**Ingredients:**\\n\\n**For the Paneer:**\\n\\n*   250g Paneer (Indian Cheese), cut into 1-inch cubes\\n*   1 tbsp Oil for shallow frying (optional, but recommended for better texture)\\n\\n**For the Tomato-Cashew Gravy:**\\n\\n*   2 tbsp Butter\\n*   1 medium Onion, finely chopped\\n*   1 tbsp Ginger-Garlic Paste\\n*   2 medium Tomatoes, roughly chopped\\n*   1/2 cup Cashews\\n*   1-2 Green Chilies, slit (adjust to taste)\\n*   1/2 tsp Turmeric Powder\\n*   1 tsp Red Chili Powder (adjust to taste)\\n*   1 tsp Coriander Powder\\n*   1/2 tsp Garam Masala\\n*   1/2 tsp Dried Fenugreek Leaves (Kasuri Methi), crushed\\n*   1/2 cup Water (or as needed to blend)\\n*   1/2 cup Heavy Cream or Fresh Cream\\n*   Salt to taste\\n*   Sugar (a pinch, optional to balance acidity)\\n\\n**For the Butter Masala Sauce:**\\n\\n*   2 tbsp Butter\\n*   1/2 tsp Red Chili Powder (for color and flavor)\\n*   1/4 tsp Garam Masala\\n*   1 tbsp Fresh Cream\\n*   Fresh Coriander Leaves, chopped for garnish\\n\\n**Instructions:**\\n\\n**1. Prepare the Paneer (Optional):**\\n\\n*   Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Gently shallow fry the paneer cubes until lightly golden brown on all sides. This helps to prevent them from crumbling in the gravy.\\n*   Remove the paneer and set aside. You can also skip this step and add the paneer directly to the gravy, but frying improves texture.\\n\\n**2. Make the Tomato-Cashew Gravy:**\\n\\n*   Heat 2 tbsp butter in a pan or pot over medium heat.\\n*   Add the chopped onion and sauté until golden brown, about 5-7 minutes.\\n*   Add the ginger-garlic paste and green chilies and sauté for another minute until fragrant.\\n*   Add the chopped tomatoes, cashews, turmeric powder, red chili powder, coriander powder, and salt. Mix well.\\n*   Add 1/2 cup water and bring to a simmer. Cover and cook for 10-15 minutes, or until the tomatoes are soft and mushy.\\n*   Let the mixture cool slightly.\\n*   Transfer the mixture to a blender or food processor and blend until smooth. If needed, add a little more water to achieve a smooth consistency.\\n*   Strain the blended gravy through a fine-mesh sieve back into the pan. This step ensures a silky smooth gravy.\\n\\n**3. Make the Butter Masala Sauce:**\\n\\n*   Return the strained gravy to the pan. Heat over low heat.\\n*   Add 2 tbsp butter, red chili powder (for color), garam masala, and crushed kasuri methi. Mix well and simmer for 2-3 minutes.\\n*   Add the shallow fried (or unfried) paneer cubes to the gravy. Gently stir to coat the paneer with the sauce.\\n*   Add the heavy cream (or fresh cream) and a pinch of sugar (if using). Mix gently and simmer for another 2-3 minutes, allowing the paneer to absorb the flavors.\\n*   Adjust salt to taste.\\n\\n**4. Serve:**\\n\\n*   Garnish with fresh coriander leaves and a swirl of fresh cream.\\n*   Serve hot with naan, roti, rice, or paratha.\\n\\n**Tips and Variations:**\\n\\n*   **Paneer Quality:** Use fresh, good-quality paneer for the best results. If using store-bought paneer, soak it in warm water for 15-20 minutes to soften it before using.\\n*   **Cashews:** Soaking the cashews in warm water for 30 minutes before blending will help them blend more smoothly.\\n*   **Cream:** Heavy cream will give a richer flavor, but fresh cream works well too. You can also use half-and-half for a lighter option.\\n*   **Spice Level:** Adjust the amount of red chili powder and green chilies to your preferred spice level.\\n*   **Smoked Flavor:** For a smoky flavor, you can give the gravy a "dhungar" treatment. Place a small steel bowl in the center of the pan of gravy. Heat a piece of charcoal on a direct flame until it\\\'s red hot. Place the hot charcoal in the bowl, add a few drops of oil or ghee to the charcoal, and immediately cover the pan with a lid. Let it smoke for 2-3 minutes. Remove the bowl and discard the charcoal.\\n*   **Vegan Option:** Substitute the paneer with tofu (press the tofu to remove excess water before using). Use vegan butter and cashew cream instead of dairy cream.\\n*   **Ginger-Garlic Paste:** You can use store-bought ginger-garlic paste, but freshly made paste will have a better flavor.\\n\\nEnjoy your delicious homemade Paneer Butter Masala!\', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={\'prompt_feedback\': {\'block_reason\': 0, \'safety_ratings\': []}, \'finish_reason\': \'STOP\', \'model_name\': \'gemini-2.0-flash\', \'safety_ratings\': []}, name=\'You are llm expert\', id=\'run-832c6624-8349-4eda-940d-e538849a0015-0\', usage_metadata={\'input_tokens\': 1789, \'output_tokens\': 1139, \'total_tokens\': 2928, \'input_token_details\': {\'cache_read\': 0}}), HumanMessage(content=\'what is \', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id=\'dcbd3b29-7783-4bfe-9131-00516d675c38\')]\n        Consider previous human feedback to refine the response.\n        If the user’s question is incomplete or ambiguous, always call the human_feedback tool to request more information before answering.\n        ', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b0e17b97-a7ed-4de8-b8d7-f447f77f4d0d'), AIMessage(content='', additional_kwargs={'function_call': {'name': 'human_feedback', 'arguments': '{"message": "Machine learning what? Please provide more details about what you\'d like to know about machine learning."}'}}, response_metadata={'prompt_feedback': {'block_reason': 0, 'safety_ratings': []}, 'finish_reason': 'STOP', 'model_name': 'gemini-2.0-flash', 'safety_ratings': []}, name='You are llm expert', id='run-a4ff3c8e-7a37-4a1b-9afc-6d6b05e8336a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'human_feedback', 'args': {'message': "Machine learning what? Please provide more details about what you'd like to know about machine learning."}, 'id': '4c37637f-9f14-45e3-a1ba-6369bfbac6f5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1977, 'output_tokens': 24, 'total_tokens': 2001, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 0}}), ToolMessage(content='Deep learning give me in deatil', name='human_feedback', id='39628287-e558-43dd-a88c-cc38a117c6dc', tool_call_id='4c37637f-9f14-45e3-a1ba-6369bfbac6f5'), AIMessage(content='', additional_kwargs={'function_call': {'name': 'general_AI', 'arguments': '{"message": "Deep learning in detail"}'}}, response_metadata={'prompt_feedback': {'block_reason': 0, 'safety_ratings': []}, 'finish_reason': 'STOP', 'model_name': 'gemini-2.0-flash', 'safety_ratings': []}, name='You are llm expert', id='run-d9e21fbb-4633-45b3-9775-5eb05e20b3d1-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'general_AI', 'args': {'message': 'Deep learning in detail'}, 'id': '611211cb-6240-4f9e-aa59-4311c6890189', 'type': 'tool_call'}], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2012, 'output_tokens': 8, 'total_tokens': 2020, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 0}}), ToolMessage(content='content=\'**Deep Learning: A Comprehensive Overview**\\n\\nDeep learning is a subset of machine learning that involves the use of neural networks with multiple layers to analyze and interpret data. The term "deep" refers to the fact that these networks have multiple layers of interconnected nodes or "neurons," which allow them to learn complex patterns and relationships in data.\\n\\n**History of Deep Learning**\\n\\nThe concept of deep learning dates back to the 1940s and 1950s, when researchers such as Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts proposed the idea of artificial neural networks. However, it wasn\\\'t until the 1980s and 1990s that the field of deep learning began to take shape.\\n\\n**Key Concepts in Deep Learning**\\n\\n1. **Neural Networks**: A neural network is a mathematical model that is composed of layers of interconnected nodes or "neurons." Each neuron applies an activation function to the weighted sum of the inputs it receives from the previous layer.\\n2. **Deep Learning Architectures**: Deep learning architectures are designed to take advantage of the hierarchical structure of data. Common architectures include:\\n\\t* **Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)**: CNNs are designed for image and video data and use convolutional and pooling layers to extract features.\\n\\t* **Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs)**: RNNs are designed for sequential data such as text and speech and use recurrent layers to capture temporal relationships.\\n\\t* **Autoencoders**: Autoencoders are neural networks that are trained to reconstruct the input data, which can be used for dimensionality reduction and anomaly detection.\\n3. **Activation Functions**: Activation functions are used to introduce non-linearity into the neural network, allowing it to learn more complex patterns. Common activation functions include:\\n\\t* **Sigmoid**: The sigmoid function maps the input to a value between 0 and 1.\\n\\t* **ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit)**: The ReLU function maps all negative values to 0 and all positive values to the input value.\\n\\t* **Tanh (Hyperbolic Tangent)**: The tanh function maps the input to a value between -1 and 1.\\n4. **Backpropagation**: Backpropagation is an algorithm used to train neural networks by minimizing the error between the predicted output and the actual output.\\n\\n**Types of Deep Learning**\\n\\n1. **Supervised Learning**: Supervised learning involves training a neural network on labeled data, where the output is a target value.\\n2. **Unsupervised Learning**: Unsupervised learning involves training a neural network on unlabeled data, where the output is not a target value.\\n3. **Reinforcement Learning**: Reinforcement learning involves training a neural network to make decisions based on rewards or penalties.\\n\\n**Applications of Deep Learning**\\n\\n1. **Computer Vision**: Deep learning has been widely used in computer vision for applications such as image classification, object detection, and segmentation.\\n2. **Natural Language Processing (NLP)**: Deep learning has been used in NLP for applications such as language translation, text classification, and sentiment analysis.\\n3. **Speech Recognition**: Deep learning has been used in speech recognition for applications such as speech-to-text and voice assistants.\\n4. **Robotics**: Deep learning has been used in robotics for applications such as robot control and computer vision.\\n\\n**Challenges in Deep Learning**\\n\\n1. **Overfitting**: Overfitting occurs when a neural network is too complex and fits the training data too closely, leading to poor performance on test data.\\n2. **Underfitting**: Underfitting occurs when a neural network is too simple and fails to capture the underlying patterns in the data.\\n3. **Vanishing Gradients**: Vanishing gradients occur when the gradients of the loss function are too small, making it difficult to train the network.\\n4. **High Computational Requirements**: Deep learning requires significant computational resources, which can be a challenge for large-scale applications.\\n\\n**Tools and Frameworks for Deep Learning**\\n\\n1. **TensorFlow**: TensorFlow is an open-source software library for machine learning.\\n2. **PyTorch**: PyTorch is an open-source software library for machine learning.\\n3. **Keras**: Keras is a high-level neural networks API that can run on top of TensorFlow, PyTorch, or Theano.\\n4. **Caffe**: Caffe is a deep learning framework that provides a simple and efficient way to train deep neural networks.\\n\\n**Real-World Examples of Deep Learning**\\n\\n1. **Image Classification**: Image classification is a classic application of deep learning, where a neural network is trained to classify images into different categories.\\n2. **Face Recognition**: Face recognition is a popular application of deep learning, where a neural network is trained to recognize faces and detect emotions.\\n3. **Language Translation**: Language translation is a challenging application of deep learning, where a neural network is trained to translate text from one language to another.\\n4. **Autonomous Vehicles**: Autonomous vehicles use deep learning to detect objects, navigate through roads, and make decisions in real-time.\\n\\n**Conclusion**\\n\\nDeep learning is a powerful tool for analyzing and interpreting complex data. With the advent of large-scale computing and the development of new algorithms and architectures, deep learning has become a critical component of many applications, including computer vision, NLP, speech recognition, and robotics. While there are challenges to deep learning, such as overfitting and vanishing gradients, the benefits of deep learning make it a valuable tool for many applications.\\n\\n**Code Example:**\\n\\nHere is an example of a simple neural network implemented using PyTorch:\\n```python\\nimport torch\\nimport torch.nn as nn\\nimport torch.optim as optim\\n\\n# Define the neural network architecture\\nclass Net(nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        super(Net, self).__init__()\\n        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(784, 128)  # input layer (28x28 images) -> hidden layer (128 units)\\n        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(128, 10)  # hidden layer (128 units) -> output layer (10 units)\\n\\n    def forward(self, x):\\n        x = torch.relu(self.fc1(x))  # activation function for the hidden layer\\n        x = self.fc2(x)\\n        return x\\n\\n# Initialize the neural network, loss function, and optimizer\\nnet = Net()\\ncriterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()\\noptimizer = optim.SGD(net.parameters(), lr=0.01)\\n\\n# Train the neural network\\nfor epoch in range(10):  # loop over the dataset multiple times\\n    running_loss = 0.0\\n    for i, data in enumerate(train_loader, 0):\\n        inputs, labels = data\\n        optimizer.zero_grad()\\n        outputs = net(inputs)\\n        loss = criterion(outputs, labels)\\n        loss.backward()\\n        optimizer.step()\\n        running_loss += loss.item()\\n    print(\\\'Epoch %d, Loss: %.3f\\\' % (epoch+1, running_loss/(i+1)))\\n```\\nThis code defines a simple neural network with two fully connected layers and trains it on the MNIST dataset using stochastic gradient descent and the cross-entropy loss function.\' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={\'token_usage\': {\'completion_tokens\': 1460, \'prompt_tokens\': 39, \'total_tokens\': 1499, \'completion_time\': 2.339159578, \'prompt_time\': 0.001950293, \'queue_time\': 0.049130197, \'total_time\': 2.341109871}, \'model_name\': \'llama-3.1-8b-instant\', \'system_fingerprint\': \'fp_55d70a61e4\', \'finish_reason\': \'stop\', \'logprobs\': None} id=\'run-dd76d32e-82a4-4b6b-b167-e6ad9d542fbc-0\' usage_metadata={\'input_tokens\': 39, \'output_tokens\': 1460, \'total_tokens\': 1499}', name='general_AI', id='4502b16f-0787-4382-b420-cc71ab7ee411', tool_call_id='611211cb-6240-4f9e-aa59-4311c6890189'), AIMessage(content='Deep learning is a subset of machine learning that uses neural networks with multiple layers to analyze data. These "deep" networks learn complex patterns. It has applications in computer vision, natural language processing, speech recognition and robotics. Some challenges include overfitting, underfitting, and high computational requirements. Frameworks like TensorFlow and PyTorch are used in deep learning.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={'prompt_feedback': {'block_reason': 0, 'safety_ratings': []}, 'finish_reason': 'STOP', 'model_name': 'gemini-2.0-flash', 'safety_ratings': []}, name='You are llm expert', id='run-665bd564-1c23-4a04-9b35-1a9fd88ad9b0-0', usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3894, 'output_tokens': 71, 'total_tokens': 3965, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 0}})]}
